# Cloudflare Pages picks this file up automatically when it sits in `dist/`.
# Vite copies `public/_headers` → `dist/_headers` at build time, so any
# change here ships with the next `pages deploy`.
#
# Header strategy (mirrors issue #474's own test plan):
#   - Enforce the cheap, non-breaking headers (MIME-sniff, framing, referrer,
#     permissions, HSTS). These have no runtime surface and apply to every
#     request.
#   - Ship CSP in `Report-Only` first. Browsers log violations to the console
#     without blocking, so a regression in Privy / WalletConnect / a future
#     transitive dep can't take prod down. After a week of clean reports the
#     `-Report-Only` suffix gets dropped and CSP becomes enforced (tracked as
#     a follow-up — see the PR closing #474).
#
# CSP `frame-src` allowlist follows Privy's own published recommendation
# (https://docs.privy.io/security/implementation-guide/content-security-policy).
# WalletConnect entries stay in even though `walletConnect.enabled: false`
# in `App.tsx` — Privy's SDK probes those origins regardless, so omitting
# them surfaces as false-positive violations.
#
# `connect-src` and `img-src` use scheme-only allowlists (`https:` / `wss:`)
# because the API host, Ponder URL, WS host, and R2 image host are all
# `VITE_*` env vars injected at build time — a static `_headers` file
# can't enumerate them. Locking the scheme still blocks the two real
# attack shapes (`http:` mixed-content exfil, `data:`/`javascript:`
# script-loaded-as-image), and `script-src 'self'` is what actually
# stops a moderation-bypassed SVG from executing inline `<script>`.
#
# `Alt-Svc: clear`: the zone-level Speed → Protocol Optimization →
# HTTP/3 toggle is off, but Cloudflare Pages emits `alt-svc: h3=":443";
# ma=86400` regardless (the toggle only affects Workers / origin-proxied
# traffic, not Pages-served responses) — there is no Pages-side opt-out
# in the dashboard. The `h3` advertisement caches in the browser's
# Alt-Svc store for 24h and steers subsequent HTTPS requests at QUIC.
# On networks where UDP/443 is blocked, filtered, or aggressively rate-
# limited (UK ISPs are a known offender — same regulatory environment
# that drives the `GB` entry in `DEFAULT_BLOCKED` in
# `src/hooks/useIsGeoBlocked.ts`), the QUIC handshake hangs silently
# and the browser parks every API call in
# "Stalled" for minutes before falling back to TCP. Setting the header
# to the special token `clear` (RFC 7838 §3.1) actively invalidates the
# browser's cached alternative-service entries for this origin in
# addition to suppressing future advertisements, so users who already
# have a poisoned cache from earlier visits get unstuck on their next
# load instead of waiting out the 24h TTL.
#
# Set it on `alt.fun` only (this file ships with the web project).
# `api.alt.fun` is on a separate zone whose toggle is already off, and
# its Workers responses already do not include the header.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  Alt-Svc: clear
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com data:; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; connect-src 'self' https: wss:; frame-src 'self' https://auth.privy.io https://verify.walletconnect.com https://verify.walletconnect.org https://challenges.cloudflare.com; worker-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; manifest-src 'self'
